1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,997
Here's a grid of twenty five dots.

2
00:00:01,997 --> 00:00:04,841
Nothing special. But there's
something hidden here,

3
00:00:04,841 --> 00:00:07,187
too small to see from this distance.

4
00:00:07,187 --> 00:00:08,824
Let's go and look.

5
00:00:08,824 --> 00:00:12,260
There it is: a message,
tucked under the middle dot,

6
00:00:12,260 --> 00:00:15,221
about the size of a grain
of sand on this screen.

7
00:00:15,221 --> 00:00:19,052
In every scene so far, the
camera has been bolted down,

8
00:00:19,052 --> 00:00:20,817
and we moved the objects.

9
00:00:20,817 --> 00:00:23,336
This episode, the camera gets to move,

10
00:00:23,336 --> 00:00:27,917
first across the flat plane, and
then into the third dimension.

11
00:00:27,917 --> 00:00:32,422
In a moving camera scene, the
camera's view is itself a mobject:

12
00:00:32,422 --> 00:00:34,999
a rectangle called the frame.

13
00:00:34,999 --> 00:00:39,016
Whatever is inside the
rectangle fills the screen.

14
00:00:39,016 --> 00:00:41,280
And because the frame is a mobject,

15
00:00:41,280 --> 00:00:44,078
everything you already know works on it.

16
00:00:44,078 --> 00:00:46,632
Scale it down by a factor
of zero point one five,

17
00:00:46,632 --> 00:00:49,303
and you zoom in by almost seven times.

18
00:00:49,303 --> 00:00:52,321
Move it to a dot, and
the view glides there.

19
00:00:52,321 --> 00:00:55,862
Animate those changes, and
you get a smooth camera move,

20
00:00:55,862 --> 00:00:57,847
with rate functions and everything.

21
00:00:57,847 --> 00:00:59,693
Save state before you go wandering,

22
00:00:59,693 --> 00:01:02,642
and restore takes you smoothly
back to where you started.

23
00:01:02,642 --> 00:01:05,962
That pattern, save, zoom in, explain,

24
00:01:05,962 --> 00:01:08,981
restore, is one you'll use constantly.

25
00:01:08,981 --> 00:01:10,664
One detail you might notice:

26
00:01:10,664 --> 00:01:12,847
when you zoom in, lines get thicker.

27
00:01:12,847 --> 00:01:16,527
That's because line widths are
measured in the scene's own units,

28
00:01:16,527 --> 00:01:19,221
so a zoomed-in camera sees them bigger,

29
00:01:19,221 --> 00:01:22,820
the way a magnifying glass
makes ink lines look fatter.

30
00:01:22,820 --> 00:01:24,816
If you need hairlines at high zoom,

31
00:01:24,816 --> 00:01:27,550
give those objects thinner strokes.

32
00:01:27,550 --> 00:01:30,720
Sometimes you want to see the detail
and the big picture at the same time.

33
00:01:30,720 --> 00:01:32,693
That's what a zoomed scene is for.

34
00:01:32,693 --> 00:01:35,735
It adds a second camera,
with its own small frame,

35
00:01:35,735 --> 00:01:40,019
and shows what that camera sees in
a display box elsewhere on screen.

36
00:01:40,019 --> 00:01:43,897
Here the small frame is told,
with an updater from episode six,

37
00:01:43,897 --> 00:01:45,952
to always sit on the dot.

38
00:01:45,952 --> 00:01:48,239
As the dot slides along the ruler,

39
00:01:48,239 --> 00:01:51,629
the inset shows a close-up of
the ticks it's passing over,

40
00:01:51,629 --> 00:01:54,857
while the main view still
shows the whole ruler.

41
00:01:54,857 --> 00:01:59,083
The zoom factor sets how much of
the scene the small frame covers.

42
00:01:59,083 --> 00:02:03,692
Zero point two five means it's a
quarter of the size of the display box,

43
00:02:03,692 --> 00:02:07,717
so everything in the inset
looks four times larger.

44
00:02:07,717 --> 00:02:10,561
For three dimensions,
we use a three D scene.

45
00:02:10,561 --> 00:02:12,326
Points now use their third number:

46
00:02:12,326 --> 00:02:13,986
depth, pointing out of the screen,

47
00:02:13,986 --> 00:02:15,089
called z.

48
00:02:15,089 --> 00:02:17,783
Where the camera sits is
described by two angles,

49
00:02:17,783 --> 00:02:19,525
like a position on a globe.

50
00:02:19,525 --> 00:02:23,124
Phi is how far the camera is
tilted from looking straight down.

51
00:02:23,124 --> 00:02:25,690
At zero, you look straight
down on the x y plane,

52
00:02:25,690 --> 00:02:28,685
and everything looks flat, just
like the scenes we've been making.

53
00:02:28,685 --> 00:02:31,378
At ninety degrees, you're
looking at it edge-on,

54
00:02:31,378 --> 00:02:32,469
from the side.

55
00:02:32,469 --> 00:02:34,896
Theta is how far round you've walked,

56
00:02:34,896 --> 00:02:36,974
measured around the vertical axis.

57
00:02:36,974 --> 00:02:40,318
Changing theta is like walking
in a circle around a sculpture.

58
00:02:40,318 --> 00:02:43,951
Set camera orientation puts the
camera anywhere on that globe.

59
00:02:43,951 --> 00:02:48,572
Seventy degrees of tilt and minus
fifty degrees around is a classic view:

60
00:02:48,572 --> 00:02:52,400
you can see depth, but the
floor is still clearly a floor.

61
00:02:52,400 --> 00:02:56,336
Everything you know about positions
carries over to three dimensions.

62
00:02:56,336 --> 00:03:00,248
Three D axes work like ordinary
axes with a third ruler,

63
00:03:00,248 --> 00:03:02,721
and c to p now takes three numbers.

64
00:03:02,721 --> 00:03:06,738
The point two, one, one
point five is two along x,

65
00:03:06,738 --> 00:03:09,397
one along y, and one and a half up.

66
00:03:09,397 --> 00:03:11,475
Directions gain two new members:

67
00:03:11,475 --> 00:03:14,935
OUT, toward the viewer when
the camera looks straight down,

68
00:03:14,935 --> 00:03:19,927
and IN, away. So UP times two plus
OUT is a perfectly good point.

69
00:03:19,927 --> 00:03:22,435
Most flat shapes still work in 3D;

70
00:03:22,435 --> 00:03:24,432
they simply lie flat in space,

71
00:03:24,432 --> 00:03:25,953
like paper cut-outs.

72
00:03:25,953 --> 00:03:28,693
For objects that should
look solid from every angle,

73
00:03:28,693 --> 00:03:30,167
there are 3D versions:

74
00:03:30,167 --> 00:03:33,917
Dot three D is a little
sphere instead of a disc.

75
00:03:33,917 --> 00:03:36,378
A function of two inputs, u and v,

76
00:03:36,378 --> 00:03:38,944
gives you a height for
every point on the floor.

77
00:03:38,944 --> 00:03:41,405
Plot surface turns that into a landscape.

78
00:03:41,405 --> 00:03:42,903
Like a plot in two dimensions,

79
00:03:42,903 --> 00:03:44,830
it can't draw the function exactly,

80
00:03:44,830 --> 00:03:49,103
so it samples it. Resolution twenty
by twenty means a grid of four hundred

81
00:03:49,103 --> 00:03:52,226
little patches, each a
flat four-sided face.

82
00:03:52,226 --> 00:03:54,350
Raise the resolution
for a smoother surface,

83
00:03:54,350 --> 00:03:56,150
at the cost of more work.

84
00:03:56,150 --> 00:03:59,145
Colorscale paints each patch
according to its height:

85
00:03:59,145 --> 00:04:01,177
blue in the valleys, through green,

86
00:04:01,177 --> 00:04:02,547
to yellow on the peaks.

87
00:04:02,547 --> 00:04:06,483
And begin ambient camera rotation
sets the camera slowly orbiting,

88
00:04:06,483 --> 00:04:11,022
which is often the single best way to
help viewers understand a 3D shape.

89
00:04:11,022 --> 00:04:15,600
Movement gives the brain depth
cues that a still image can't.

90
00:04:15,600 --> 00:04:18,026
Manim includes ready-made solids:

91
00:04:18,026 --> 00:04:21,521
spheres, cubes, tori, cones, cylinders,

92
00:04:21,521 --> 00:04:24,842
and more. Each one is
built from flat faces,

93
00:04:24,842 --> 00:04:26,212
just like the surface.

94
00:04:26,212 --> 00:04:28,174
Two things make them look solid.

95
00:04:28,174 --> 00:04:33,073
First, shading: each face is lit
according to which way it points,

96
00:04:33,073 --> 00:04:35,639
so faces turned away from
the light are darker.

97
00:04:35,639 --> 00:04:37,404
Second, depth sorting:

98
00:04:37,404 --> 00:04:41,421
before drawing a frame, faces are put
in order from farthest to nearest,

99
00:04:41,421 --> 00:04:45,194
and painted in that order,
so near faces cover far ones.

100
00:04:45,194 --> 00:04:47,238
Painters have worked
this way for centuries,

101
00:04:47,238 --> 00:04:50,733
which is why it's often called
the painter's algorithm.

102
00:04:50,733 --> 00:04:54,158
Here's a helix: a spiral
that climbs as it turns,

103
00:04:54,158 --> 00:04:56,468
like a spring or a spiral staircase.

104
00:04:56,468 --> 00:04:59,011
Its points are cosine of t, sine of t,

105
00:04:59,011 --> 00:05:00,288
and t over four.

106
00:05:00,288 --> 00:05:02,471
Notice the title. In a 3D scene,

107
00:05:02,471 --> 00:05:04,514
ordinary objects tilt with the world.

108
00:05:04,514 --> 00:05:06,732
But titles and labels usually shouldn't,

109
00:05:06,732 --> 00:05:10,760
so add fixed in frame mobjects
pins them flat to the screen,

110
00:05:10,760 --> 00:05:12,119
like a caption on a window.

111
00:05:12,119 --> 00:05:14,290
Now watch what move camera reveals.

112
00:05:14,290 --> 00:05:16,670
From straight above, the
climbing doesn't show at all,

113
00:05:16,670 --> 00:05:18,412
and the helix is just a circle.

114
00:05:18,412 --> 00:05:20,606
From the side, the circling doesn't show,

115
00:05:20,606 --> 00:05:22,231
and the helix is a wave.

116
00:05:22,231 --> 00:05:25,621
In fact, it's exactly the
sine wave from episode six.

117
00:05:25,621 --> 00:05:29,580
That point going round a circle and the
wave it traced were always two shadows

118
00:05:29,580 --> 00:05:31,983
of this one spiral.

119
00:05:31,983 --> 00:05:35,942
A 3D scene is only as clear
as the view you choose,

120
00:05:35,942 --> 00:05:38,310
and some views hide everything.

121
00:05:38,310 --> 00:05:41,108
Straight down, a surface looks flat.

122
00:05:41,108 --> 00:05:45,253
Straight from the side, it
collapses to a single wavy line.

123
00:05:45,253 --> 00:05:47,552
From underneath, nothing makes sense.

124
00:05:47,552 --> 00:05:50,373
A tilt of sixty to seventy five degrees,

125
00:05:50,373 --> 00:05:52,869
and a turn of thirty to
sixty degrees around,

126
00:05:52,869 --> 00:05:54,704
is a reliable starting point:

127
00:05:54,704 --> 00:05:57,966
it shows depth while keeping
the floor recognisable.

128
00:05:57,966 --> 00:05:59,905
Motion helps, but gently.

129
00:05:59,905 --> 00:06:01,937
A slow orbit reveals shape.

130
00:06:01,937 --> 00:06:05,432
A fast spin makes people
dizzy and hides detail.

131
00:06:05,432 --> 00:06:07,405
And when you want viewers
to read something,

132
00:06:07,405 --> 00:06:08,926
stop the camera first.

133
00:06:08,926 --> 00:06:11,991
A good rhythm is: establish a wide view,

134
00:06:11,991 --> 00:06:14,209
move slowly to where the action is,

135
00:06:14,209 --> 00:06:17,500
hold still, and only then point at things.

136
00:06:17,500 --> 00:06:21,552
A brief look under the hood, because
it's a nice piece of engineering.

137
00:06:21,552 --> 00:06:25,604
In ManimOnline, Python doesn't
flatten the 3D world itself.

138
00:06:25,604 --> 00:06:29,203
Each frame records the 3D
shapes and where the camera is,

139
00:06:29,203 --> 00:06:30,933
and the browser does the shading,

140
00:06:30,933 --> 00:06:33,081
sorting and projecting when it draws.

141
00:06:33,081 --> 00:06:35,716
That split pays off when
something moves rigidly,

142
00:06:35,716 --> 00:06:37,075
like a spinning cube.

143
00:06:37,075 --> 00:06:39,884
Instead of sending the cube's
points again for every frame,

144
00:06:39,884 --> 00:06:42,625
a frame can say: "the same cube as before,

145
00:06:42,625 --> 00:06:44,285
turned by this much".

146
00:06:44,285 --> 00:06:47,933
Episode ten looks at the
rest of that machinery.

147
00:06:47,933 --> 00:06:51,149
The camera is a mobject
too, so you can move it,

148
00:06:51,149 --> 00:06:53,227
zoom it, save it and restore it.

149
00:06:53,227 --> 00:06:55,967
A zoomed scene adds a magnifying inset.

150
00:06:55,967 --> 00:06:59,891
In three dimensions, phi and
theta place the camera on a globe,

151
00:06:59,891 --> 00:07:03,072
surfaces and solids are
made of flat shaded faces,

152
00:07:03,072 --> 00:07:06,811
and fixed-in-frame text stays
readable while the world turns.

153
00:07:06,811 --> 00:07:09,307
That completes our tour
of what you can draw.

154
00:07:09,307 --> 00:07:12,465
Next time, we turn to the
practical side of ManimOnline:

155
00:07:12,465 --> 00:07:14,194
the gallery, exporting videos,

156
00:07:14,194 --> 00:07:15,971
sharing links, saving your work,

157
00:07:15,971 --> 00:07:17,608
and its two other languages.
